WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.900
The recent indictment of Alec Baldwin&nbsp;
has brought to light numerous other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.440
incidents in which movie people&nbsp;
were taken into custody. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.967
are just a few of the most notorious and&nbsp;
unusual cases of on-set arrests to date.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:19.260
In 2021, cinematographer Halyna Hutchins was&nbsp;
killed when a prop gun held by actor Alec Baldwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:24.480
discharged during the making of the Western&nbsp;
movie Rust. The accident drew intense scrutiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.420
by both legal and media professionals, who opined&nbsp;
furiously over issues of set safety and debated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.460
who bears responsibility for the accident. Baldwin&nbsp;
was handed what he believed to be a "cold gun,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:41.280
or one without live ammunition. He claimed no&nbsp;
responsibility for the shooting, sought to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.468
financial responsibility for Hutchins' death, and&nbsp;
even sued crew members over claims of negligence.

00:00:46.468 --> 00:00:49.303
"I think with Rust it was a&nbsp;
perfect storm of, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:53.954
the armorer, the assistant director, the&nbsp;
culture that was on set, the rushing."

00:00:53.954 --> 00:00:57.286
The Times quoted investigators'&nbsp;
interviews with the film's armorer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:01.666
Hannah Gutierrez-Reed, who claimed she&nbsp;
loaded and checked the gun, but added,

00:01:01.793 --> 00:01:03.473
"I wish I would've checked it more."

00:01:03.473 --> 00:01:09.420
In January 2023, prosecutors in New Mexico, where&nbsp;
the accident occurred, announced they sought to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.940
charge Baldwin and Gutierrez-Reed with involuntary&nbsp;
manslaughter. Officials revealed that the prop gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.301
involved in the shooting could not have discharged&nbsp;
ammunition without its trigger being pulled.

00:01:19.301 --> 00:01:21.380
"The trigger wasn't pulled.&nbsp;
I didn't pull the trigger."

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.560
Baldwin has vehemently denied doing so,&nbsp;
but Santa Fe County district attorney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.480
Mary Carmack-Altwies has claimed that Baldwin&nbsp;
should have properly checked the gun. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.980
Baldwin's assertion that actors have never&nbsp;
been responsible for gun safety on sets is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.240
backed by the actors union, SAG-AFTRA.&nbsp;
The union issued a statement that noted,

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.480
"An actor's job is not to be&nbsp;
a firearms or weapons expert."

00:01:42.797 --> 00:01:45.060
The Rust case remains ongoing.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.440
While filming a segment for the 1983&nbsp;
anthology film Twilight Zone: The Movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.640
actor Vic Morrow and two child performers&nbsp;
were killed during a sequence in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.020
Morrow's character and the children run&nbsp;
to a waiting helicopter. The chopper was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.580
incapacitated by special effects explosions&nbsp;
and plummeted to the ground, killing Morrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.600
six-year-old Renee Shin-Yi Chen,&nbsp;
and seven-year-old Myca Dinh Le.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.860
The tragedy led to indictments for involuntary&nbsp;
manslaughter for director John Landis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.480
the helicopter's pilot, Dorcey Wingo, and&nbsp;
three others involved with the film. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.160
10-month trial was closely followed by the media&nbsp;
and members of the film industry — and in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.900
all five defendants were acquitted. Landis&nbsp;
went on to direct Coming to America and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.216
other projects — though for some defendants,&nbsp;
the case spelled the end of their careers.

00:02:29.216 --> 00:02:32.700
"I'm sorry. I think it's inappropriate&nbsp;
for me, at this time, to say anything." 
 &nbsp;

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.860
The deadly Twilight Zone accident inspired change&nbsp;
in the industry. John Silva, a vice-president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.780
at Warner Bros., which released Twilight Zone,&nbsp;
created a committee to establish on-set safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.040
standards. With the help of the unions, Silva and&nbsp;
the committee laid the groundwork for the Injury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.620
and Illness Prevention Program (IIPP), a set of&nbsp;
guidelines issued to studios and regularly updated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.900
to reflect developing standards. Though injuries&nbsp;
and even fatalities have continued to occur on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.940
movie sets, the IIPP has led to greater attention&nbsp;
to safety in the film and television workplace.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.400
Although he's known today as an Oscar-winning&nbsp;
actor and filmmaker, as well as an outspoken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.440
social and political activist, Sean Penn's early&nbsp;
career was marked by altercations with the media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.480
and the public. The mid-1980s, in particular, saw&nbsp;
Penn charged with misdemeanor assault and battery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.100
on several occasions, including a fight with an&nbsp;
extra on the set of the Dennis Hopper-directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.620
film Colors in 1987. An extra on the film&nbsp;
photographed Penn and co-star Robert Duvall during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.900
a break in filming, but Penn reportedly objected&nbsp;
and spat at him before striking him several times.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.800
The assault charge violated Penn's&nbsp;
probation on a previous battery charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.560
which he had already broken&nbsp;
due to misdemeanor charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.753
of reckless driving and driving under&nbsp;
the influence of alcohol in May 1987.

00:03:44.753 --> 00:03:46.888
"I did some bad things."

00:03:46.888 --> 00:03:51.180
Municipal Court Commissioner Juelann Cathey&nbsp;
sentenced the actor to 60 days in jail for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.260
the assault and reckless driving charges, and&nbsp;
imposed an additional two years' probation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.360
The incident would be Penn's last visit to&nbsp;
court until 2009, when he was given 36 months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:05.000
probation and 300 hours of community&nbsp;
service for assaulting a photographer.

00:04:05.223 --> 00:04:09.360
A chorus of mixed and dismissive reviews&nbsp;
wasn't the only indignity Ryan Gosling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.940
experienced as a result of his 2014 directorial&nbsp;
debut with the experimental feature Lost River.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.080
Gosling was arrested while scouting&nbsp;
locations for the project in Detroit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.920
Michigan, where he had recently filmed&nbsp;
George Clooney's 2011 feature The Ides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:27.360
of March. Gosling remained in Detroit after&nbsp;
that film wrapped to shoot footage of empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.413
buildings throughout the city that&nbsp;
appears in the opening of Lost River.

00:04:30.413 --> 00:04:33.060
"We're gonna be fine. We have to&nbsp;
do it. It's the right thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.636
do. And nothing bad happens when&nbsp;
you're doing the right thing."

00:04:35.636 --> 00:04:40.020
Unfortunately, Gosling ran afoul of&nbsp;
Detroit police. According to the actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.560
he was arrested for allegedly trying&nbsp;
to steal copper wire from an abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.030
location. Gosling told the Los Angeles Times,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.760
"I'm not proud that I was arrested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.540
but it did give me one of the central pieces&nbsp;
for the main character [in Lost River]."

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:55.260
In the film, Iain De Caestecker&nbsp;
plays a teenager who scrounges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.546
burnt-out buildings for wire in order&nbsp;
to help his mother make ends meet.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.480
The 2014 death of camera assistant Sarah&nbsp;
Jones on the set of Midnight Rider made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.220
director Randall Miller the only filmmaker&nbsp;
in motion picture history to receive a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.360
prison sentence for an on-set fatality.&nbsp;
Jones was struck and killed by a train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.220
on the first day of filming on Rider, a&nbsp;
biopic of rocker Gregg Allman. Charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.820
of criminal trespassing and involuntary&nbsp;
manslaughter were filed against Miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.600
his wife, producer Jody Savin, and two&nbsp;
other members of the production team.

00:05:24.706 --> 00:05:27.886
"He had been told, 'Don't go on&nbsp;
those tracks,' and he did anyway."

00:05:27.886 --> 00:05:30.000
In exchange for dropping&nbsp;
the charges against Savin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.340
Miller agreed to a plea deal that sentenced him&nbsp;
to two years in jail and eight years probation.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.700
Miller spent a year behind bars&nbsp;
before his release in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.260
though he returned to court in 2021 for&nbsp;
violating one of the terms of his probation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:48.420
which was to refrain from directing films until&nbsp;
the completion of his sentence. Miller directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.280
the 2019 feature Higher Grounds, but avoided&nbsp;
returning to prison because he thought he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.640
was only forbidden from overseeing any&nbsp;
safety precautions on the production.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.800
According to Deadline, more than 80 people have&nbsp;
died due to on-set accidents in the past hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.060
years. But prior to Miller's conviction,&nbsp;
only two people had been prosecuted on such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:11.460
charges. Pathé Film Studio vice president John&nbsp;
C. Flinn and production manager Harry Lalley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.420
who were indicted for second-degree&nbsp;
manslaughter in 1929, when a fire at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:20.520
their studio killed 10 people. The charges were&nbsp;
later dismissed by the New York Court of Appeals.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.140
Before he helped to revolutionize the&nbsp;
modern horror movie with his zombie films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.700
including Night of the Living Dead and Dawn of&nbsp;
the Dead, writer-director George A. Romero was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.080
a New York City boy dreaming of a career&nbsp;
in the movies. He was inspired in part by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.180
Michael Powell and Emeric Pressburger's vibrant&nbsp;
fantasy Tales of Hoffman, of which he later said,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.080
"That made me feel that, gee, maybe&nbsp;
I could figure this medium out."

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.960
"Everyone says, 'What?' But that's my favorite&nbsp;
film of all time. It's a fantasy film."

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.180
Like countless other would-be filmmakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.260
Romero borrowed a camera and began&nbsp;
experimenting. One of his earliest efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:01.320
an 8mm science fiction film called The Man from&nbsp;
the Meteor, led to his first arrest. He told NPR,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.680
"The man from the meteor was ultimately shot with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.860
his own ray gun and fell flaming&nbsp;
off the roof where I lived."

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.000
To get the shot, Romero lit a dummy on&nbsp;
fire and threw it off the top of a building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.940
without obtaining permission from the NYPD, who&nbsp;
quickly arrested the 14-year-old. As he recalled,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.480
"I was hauled away by the police and&nbsp;
my parents were called. It wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.302
a serious arrest. I didn't have to&nbsp;
spend the night in jail or anything."

00:07:24.302 --> 00:07:29.340
Director Ruggero Deodato's 1980&nbsp;
horror-exploitation film Cannibal Holocaust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:34.560
helped create the found-footage subgenre. It&nbsp;
presented an extremely convincing, but entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.660
fake documentary about a team of rogue filmmakers&nbsp;
tormenting indigenous people and animals in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.580
the Amazon jungle before receiving a gruesome&nbsp;
comeuppance at the hands of a cannibalistic tribe.

00:07:44.701 --> 00:07:47.581
"It's unbelievable. It's – it's horrible."

00:07:47.581 --> 00:07:51.360
The film's extreme violence, which&nbsp;
unfortunately included some real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.260
animal deaths, looked so convincing&nbsp;
to authorities in Deodato's native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:59.880
Italy that he was charged with murdering his&nbsp;
cast members and faced a 30-year sentence.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.860
Charges were dropped after Deodato produced the&nbsp;
actors who played the four slain filmmakers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:09.960
Cannibal Holocaust, though he still received a&nbsp;
four-month suspended sentence on obscenity charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:15.600
and was fined for animal cruelty. Cannibal&nbsp;
Holocaust was banned in Italy until 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.380
and remains available only in&nbsp;
edited form in many countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.889
including the United Kingdom and New Zealand.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.520
Before filmmaker John Waters inspired&nbsp;
the Tony Award-winning Broadway musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:30.840
Hairspray with his 1988 film of&nbsp;
the same name, he was known for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.809
string of transgressive underground films like&nbsp;
Female Trouble, Polyester, and Pink Flamingos.

00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:38.086
"Could you give us some of&nbsp;
your political beliefs?"

00:08:38.086 --> 00:08:41.866
"Kill everyone now. Condone first degree murder."

00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:48.000
Waters's early films skewered notions of gender,&nbsp;
class, and politics with a gleeful mix of bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.760
taste and anarchic humor, which often ran afoul&nbsp;
of censor boards. His vision landed him in hot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.860
water with the police in his hometown of Baltimore&nbsp;
during the making of his 1969 film Mondo Trasho.

00:08:58.935 --> 00:09:01.995
"I thought, 'What can we do&nbsp;
that isn't against the law yet?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.560
Something that will really freak people out."

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.180
In his hilarious 1981 memoir Shock&nbsp;
Value, Waters explained that Trasho,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.600
his first feature-length film, featured&nbsp;
a scene in which his frequent star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.540
the formidable drag performer Divine, ran over a&nbsp;
nude male hitchhiker. Waters decided to film the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:23.460
scene on the campus of Johns Hopkins University,&nbsp;
which resulted in his arrest for conspiracy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.080
commit indecent exposure and accusations that&nbsp;
he and his friends were making pornography.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.460
The ever-shrewd Waters contacted the American&nbsp;
Civil Liberties Union for his defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.360
and the resulting trial earned coverage&nbsp;
in Variety and other major publications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.320
Waters torched the district attorney's&nbsp;
case by screening one of his early films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.460
Hag in a Black Leather Jacket, which&nbsp;
proved trashy and self-consciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.480
arty but definitely not obscene.&nbsp;
Waters told the Johns Hopkins Hub,

00:09:48.713 --> 00:09:50.371
"They were so disappointed."

00:09:50.371 --> 00:09:55.500
Indie filmmaker Timothy Christian characterized&nbsp;
his decision to use a quiet neighborhood in St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.660
Paul, Minnesota as the setting for a major action&nbsp;
sequence in his 2013 action-drama, The Minnesota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:06.240
Delivery, as spur of the moment. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
Christian's burst of inspiration also caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.620
him to forget two important facts: permits are&nbsp;
required to shoot a movie in a residential area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:15.900
and homeowners tend to freak out at the sight&nbsp;
of armed strangers running amok on their lawns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:19.680
The St. Paul police were summoned to the&nbsp;
North End neighborhood on September 27,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.860
2013, where, according to investigators' reports:

00:10:23.304 --> 00:10:26.460
"[They found] four individuals&nbsp;
[...] some armed with handguns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.040
taking cover behind a vehicle&nbsp;
while pointing the weapon."

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.540
Christian and three of his actors were held&nbsp;
in jail for about 48 hours but were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.560
charged. They eventually recovered their&nbsp;
equipment, including his digital camera and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:41.301
footage of the action scene. Christian&nbsp;
told the Twin Cities Pioneer Press,

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.280
"We ended up using the shot.&nbsp;
I think it was worth it."

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.350
The footage wasn't the only souvenir&nbsp;
from his run-in with the police:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.456 --> 00:10:52.496
Christian also used a quote from a police officer&nbsp;
on the film's promotional material. It read,

00:10:52.496 --> 00:10:54.854
"It did create a lot of fear in the neighborhood."

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.240
As co-founder and CEO of Death Row Records,&nbsp;
Marion "Suge" Knight was a powerhouse in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.500
the 1990s gangsta rap scene, overseeing&nbsp;
chart-topping releases by artists like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:09.960
Dr. Dre [dray, Snoop Dogg, and Tupac Shakur.&nbsp;
Knight had a reputation for ruthlessness and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.680
was reportedly linked to the deaths&nbsp;
of Shakur and the Notorious B.I.G.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.996
two groundbreaking rappers whose&nbsp;
murders remain unsolved to this day.

00:11:17.996 --> 00:11:20.756
"Not guilty, not guilty, not guilty."

00:11:20.756 --> 00:11:25.740
In 2015, Knight was charged with murder,&nbsp;
attempted murder, and hit-and-run after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.980
an altercation with two men that began&nbsp;
on the set of Straight Outta Compton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:33.840
a feature about the influential rap group&nbsp;
N.W.A. After feuding with record label&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.460
owner Terry Carter and Cle "Bone" Sloan, a&nbsp;
consultant on Compton, Knight got in his car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:42.780
and followed the men to a restaurant near&nbsp;
the set. Video taken at the scene showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:48.480
Knight's car running over Sloan twice and then&nbsp;
hitting Carter, who later died of his injuries.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.200
Knight was out on bail in a robbery&nbsp;
case at the time of the accident that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.760
in addition to previous convictions, subjected&nbsp;
him to California's notorious "three strikes"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:02.580
law. In 2015, facing the possibility of a life&nbsp;
sentence, Knight pleaded no contest to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:07.800
manslaughter charge and agreed to serve 28 years&nbsp;
in prison. According to the California Department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:13.560
of Corrections and Rehabilitation, Knight&nbsp;
will not be eligible for parole until 2034.

